From: Jyothi Vikrama Simha Reddy Dirisinapu <jvikramsrd@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] elanmoc2: Fix double free of the retry GError in identify/verify

fpi_device_verify_report() and fpi_device_identify_report() take ownership
of the GError they are passed: both store it directly as data->error in the
task's FpMatchData, which match_data_free() later releases with
g_clear_error(). Other drivers honour this -- see virtual-device-storage.c,
which passes g_steal_pointer (&error).

elanmoc2_identify_verify_report() passed *error instead of stealing it, while
every caller holds that same error in a g_autoptr(GError). The error is
therefore freed twice: once by the autoptr when the SSM state handler
returns, and again by match_data_free() when the task data is destroyed.

The window is not narrow. When the sensor rejects a finger,
IDENTIFY_GET_FINGER_INFO takes the can_retry branch, reports the error and
jumps back to IDENTIFY_IDENTIFY -- so the free-after-free repeats on every
unrecognised touch. On an 04f3:0c00 this reliably kills fprintd after a
handful of failed verifies:

  fprintd[1684]: Device reported an error during verify: Finger not recognized
  fprintd[1684]: Driver reported an error code without setting match result to error!
  kernel: traps: fprintd[1684] general protection fault ip:... in libc.so.6
  systemd-coredump: Process 1684 (fprintd) ... dumped core.
    #3  match_data_free (libfprint-2.so.2 + 0xb439)

and on the abort path:

  fprintd[3390]: free(): invalid pointer
  systemd-coredump: Process 3390 (fprintd) ... dumped core.
    #8  match_data_free (libfprint-2.so.2 + 0xb439)

Steal the error at both report call sites so libfprint holds the only
reference.

While here, set FPI_MATCH_ERROR when an error is reported on the verify
path. Passing FPI_MATCH_FAIL alongside a non-NULL error is what produces
the "Driver reported an error code without setting match result to error!"
warning above, which precedes each crash.

The return value of elanmoc2_identify_verify_report() is not used by any
caller; it is adjusted to consistently mean "the finger matched".
---
--- i/libfprint/drivers/elanmoc2/elanmoc2.c
+++ w/libfprint/drivers/elanmoc2/elanmoc2.c
@@ -467,9 +467,14 @@
  * @error: Optional error
  *
  * Calls the correct verify or identify report function based on the input data.
- * Returns whether the action should be completed.
  *
- * Returns: Whether to complete the action.
+ * fpi_device_verify_report() and fpi_device_identify_report() take ownership of
+ * the #GError they are handed (they store it in the task's #FpMatchData and
+ * free it from match_data_free()). @error is therefore stolen and cleared here
+ * whenever it is passed on, so that a caller holding it in a g_autoptr() does
+ * not free it a second time.
+ *
+ * Returns: %TRUE if the finger matched.
  */
 static gboolean
 elanmoc2_identify_verify_report (FpDevice *device, FpPrint *print,
@@ -500,8 +505,8 @@
             }
           fp_info ("Identify: no match");
         }
-      fpi_device_identify_report (device, NULL, print, *error);
-      return TRUE;
+      fpi_device_identify_report (device, NULL, print, g_steal_pointer (error));
+      return FALSE;
     }
   else
     {
@@ -523,8 +528,13 @@
               print = NULL;
             }
         }
-      fpi_device_verify_report (device, result, print, *error);
-      return result != FPI_MATCH_FAIL;
+      /* An error must be accompanied by FPI_MATCH_ERROR, otherwise
+       * fpi_device_verify_report() warns and the result is inconsistent. */
+      if (*error != NULL)
+        result = FPI_MATCH_ERROR;
+
+      fpi_device_verify_report (device, result, print, g_steal_pointer (error));
+      return result == FPI_MATCH_SUCCESS;
     }
 }
 
